# UST.G3AU Adjoint Normal-First Obstruction Theorem

## Purpose

The strict G3AR promotion contract correctly asks for the complete
248-coordinate `beta_C` vector. A candidate that is already obstructed need
not pay that full cost. G3AU pulls the 126 normal covectors backward through
the frame and period maps, so a nonzero branch can be rejected before the full
payload is materialized.

## Exact factorization

Write the same-source maps as

```text
S : Z^1509   -> B103^248       selected period/source map,
F : B103^248 -> G3AL^248      exact frame transform,
N : G3AL^248 -> K^126         selected normal operator.
```

For a bound integral source `z`, associativity gives

```text
beta_B103 = S z,
beta_G3AL = F beta_B103,
r = N beta_G3AL = (N F) beta_B103 = (N F S) z.
```

Thus each row of `N` can be pulled back first. The computation may evaluate
the 126 normal rows adaptively and stop at the first exact nonzero or
interval-separated coordinate. That coordinate is already a complete
obstruction.

## Branch logic

The two outcomes are deliberately asymmetric:

- `r_j != 0` for any row: rigorous no-go for that bound source;
- all 126 rows vanish: the source survives the normal test, but the full
  248-row `beta_C`, exact frame binding and G3AR characteristic-zero zero
  certificate are still required for promotion.

For the finite G3AS operator, a nonzero reduced coordinate is also a rigorous
characteristic-zero no-go for an integrally normalized source. A modular zero
retains exactly its old status: necessary congruence only.

## Computational consequence

The early branch evaluates at most 126 scalar output rows rather than first
materializing 248 beta rows. A nonzero obstruction may require only one row.
This is an output/evaluation-order reduction, not a claim that every row has
equal internal cost. A surviving candidate intentionally pays the complete
G3AR cost so the proof standard is unchanged.

## Proof boundary

G3AU does not emit `S`, `F`, `beta_C`, or a normal residual. It does not alter
the G3AR promotion contract and does not promote modular zero. Its result is
an exact execution order that makes the likely no-go branch cheaper and keeps
the zero branch fully rigorous.

